.TH HSYNC-RENDEZVOUS 1 "2026-10-31" "hsync-rendezvous 1.0.14" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsync-rendezvous \- HSYNC P2P rendezvous node (no-log, zero-knowledge)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsync-rendezvous
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsync-rendezvous
is the Internet rendezvous node of the HSYNC sync network. It tells P2P clients
which peers are currently online, speaking the
.B rdv/1
protocol over QUIC/UDP port 443 (TLS 1.3 inside QUIC, mandatory RETRY and
address-validation tokens).
.PP
The routing table lives exclusively in RAM with a configurable TTL (default
60 seconds, 1\-3600): nothing peer-related is ever written to disk and no log
file is produced. The node's TLS identity (a self-signed ECDSA P\-256 key+cert)
persists by default so its fingerprint is stable across restarts and can be
pinned by clients;
.BR "run \-\-ephemeral"
keeps it in memory instead. Stopping the process erases every trace of the
network except the node's own key+cert.
.PP
The node can also relay the peers' opaque TLS by default: two peers that no
inbound NAT mapping reaches (carrier-grade NAT, a hotspot behind a carrier) can
still run their data plane through the node, which forwards the encrypted bytes
without ever terminating or inspecting them. Disable the relay with
.BR "run \-\-no\-relay" .
.PP
Diagnostics go to stderr in two channels: an always\-on, peer\-free operational
channel (\fBwarn\fP/\fBerror\fP: a UPnP mapping that could not be created, an
unwritable readiness file, an unexpected session failure) and the opt\-in full
operation log described under
.BR "run \-\-debug" ,
which is the only channel that may contain peer identity.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Run the rendezvous node (rdv/1): one QUIC/UDP listener, or several with
.BR \-\-listen\-ports .
.TP
.B identity show
Print the persisted identity fingerprint (or its certificate PEM with
.BR \-\-pem ).
.TP
.B identity rotate
Regenerate the persisted identity and print the new fingerprint. Existing DNS/IP
SANs are preserved; repeat
.B \-\-public\-host
only for new names.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--listen-host\fP= HOST [\fI:PORT\fP]
Address to bind the QUIC/UDP listener to. Accepts an optional port
.RI ( HOST [: PORT ],
e.g.
.IR 0.0.0.0:443
or
.IR [::1]:443 ).
Default:
.IR 0.0.0.0 .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--listen-port\fP= PORT
UDP port to listen on (1\-65535). Default: 443 (QUIC/HTTP3). Ports below 1024 need root or
.BR CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE .
Mutually exclusive with a port in
.BR \-\-listen\-host .
Use
.B \-\-listen\-ports
to listen on several UDP ports at once (client\-egress fallback).
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--listen-ports\fP= P1,P2,...
Comma-separated UDP ports to listen on in one process (multi-port). The
listeners share one in-memory routing table, so a peer registered via one port
is visible to a query on another. Use it to offer several inbound doors when a
client's egress firewall may block UDP 443 but allow another UDP port; the
recommended set is 443,53. Binding is best-effort: a port that cannot bind is
skipped with an always-on warning and the node fails only if none bind.
Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-listen\-port .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--identity\fP= PATH
Path of the persistent TLS identity (key+cert), created on first run and reused
afterwards, so the certificate fingerprint is stable across restarts and can be
pinned by clients. Defaults to
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_IDENTITY
or
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/hsync/rendezvous/identity.pem ,
and is created on first run. The file is written with 0600 permissions in a 0700
directory and contains no peer data. On load the key is verified against the
certificate, so a mismatched or corrupted file is refused instead of failing
later in the TLS handshake.
.TP
.B run\ \fR--ephemeral\fP
Keep the TLS identity in memory and write no identity file (read-only
filesystems, throwaway nodes); the fingerprint then changes on every restart, so
clients cannot pin it. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-identity .
Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_EPHEMERAL=1 .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--public-host\fP= NAME
Public hostname or IP to add to the certificate SAN; repeatable for multiple
names.
.TP
.BI identity\ rotate\ \fR--public-host\fP= NAME
Hostname or IP to add to the new certificate SAN when rotating; repeatable.
Existing DNS/IP SANs are preserved automatically.
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--protected-bucket\fP= NAME
Bucket requiring a valid
.I bucket_token
Repeatable for multiple buckets. Declaring a protected bucket without
provisioning its key fails fast at startup with exit code 2.
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--keys-fd\fP= FD
File descriptor carrying the group keys, one
.I bucket=<hex\-key>
line per group; blank lines and
.I #
comments are ignored. Read once at startup, never written to disk. On POSIX the
read is bounded in time, so a descriptor held open without EOF fails fast with a
configuration error instead of hanging startup.
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--keys-file\fP= PATH
File carrying the group keys in the same format as
.BR \-\-keys\-fd ,
read once at startup and never written. Portable alternative for service
managers that cannot pass a descriptor (Windows/WinSW); mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-keys\-fd .
The path must be a regular file (a symlink is refused); on POSIX a
group-writable or world\-accessible file is reported on the always\-on warning
channel (0640 group\-read is accepted).
Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_KEYS_FILE .
.TP
.B run\ \fR--private\fP
Private node: every frame must carry a valid
.I bucket_token
(applied to the whole node, not only to the buckets declared with
.BR \-\-protected\-bucket ).
A bucket without a group key is refused exactly like a wrong token, and
.I leave
is gated too (an unauthenticated leave is silently dropped). Starting a private
node with no group key fails fast with exit code 2. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_PRIVATE=1 .
.TP
.B run\ \fR--upnp\fP
Map every bound QUIC/UDP listener port with UPnP (opt\-in; off by default).
With
.BR \-\-listen\-ports ,
each bound port is mapped so every offered door is also reachable from the WAN.
.TP
.B run\ \fR--relay\fP\ /\fR--no-relay\fP
Relay the peers' opaque TLS (on by default; disable with
.BR \-\-no\-relay ).
Without the relay every relay frame is answered with
.I relay_unavailable
and only directly reachable peers connect. The node never terminates or
inspects the relayed TLS. Env form:
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY=0
disables the relay,
.B =1
forces it.
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--relay-idle-timeout\fP= SECONDS
Seconds without traffic in either direction before a relayed session (or a
listener control stream) is closed. Default: 120; range 30\-3600. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY_IDLE_TIMEOUT .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--relay-session-bytes\fP= BYTES
Close a relayed session after this many forwarded bytes (0 = no per\-session
cap; the byte rate stays the primary bound). Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY_SESSION_BYTES .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--relay-bandwidth\fP= KIB_PER_SECOND
Aggregate relay throughput cap in KiB/s (default 8192; 0 = unlimited). Excess
traffic is throttled by backpressure, never dropped. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY_BANDWIDTH .
.BI run\ \fR--ttl\fP= SECONDS
Record TTL in seconds (1\-3600). Default: 60 (peers refresh every 30 s).
.TP
.B run\ \fR--debug\fP
Emit the full operation log to stderr (opt\-in; off by default): connections,
frames, decisions, record changes and TTL sweeps, including the peer identity
(CID, bucket, IP, port) needed for analysis. Each line opens with a UTC
ISO\-8601 timestamp and the emitting process identity
(\fBts - hsync-rendezvous[pid] debug:\fP), so the stream can be ordered and
correlated with the logs of the other HSYNC components. Stderr only \- it is
never written to disk, and without it no peer data leaves memory. The always\-on
warn/error channel does not depend on this flag. Use it for the session you are
diagnosing, never as a deployment setting: the peer identity it carries would be
persisted by the log the service manager keeps. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_DEBUG=1 .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--ready-file\fP= PATH
Write the process PID to
.I PATH
once the QUIC listener is bound, and remove it on graceful shutdown (contains
no peer data). A path that cannot be written (missing directory, permissions, an
existing stale file) fails fast with exit code 4 and an actionable message. Env
form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_READY_FILE .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--metrics-host\fP= HOST
Loopback address for the metrics endpoint (default 127.0.0.1). Only a loopback
literal (127\-..., ::1) or localhost is accepted: the endpoint serves plain HTTP
and is never bound to a public interface. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_HOST .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--metrics-port\fP= PORT
Serve the aggregate Prometheus metrics on the loopback HOST:PORT (1\-65535).
Requires a bearer token (\-\-metrics\-token\-fd or \-\-metrics\-token\-file);
no endpoint is opened without both. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_PORT .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--metrics-token-fd\fP= FD
Descriptor holding the metrics bearer token: one line, at least 32 characters. It
is the endpoint's only authorization and is never passed on the command line nor
echoed. Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_TOKEN_FD .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--metrics-token-file\fP= PATH
File holding the metrics bearer token; portable alternative to
\-\-metrics\-token\-fd (the two are mutually exclusive). Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_TOKEN_FILE .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--stats-file\fP= PATH
Write the aggregate counters to PATH in Prometheus text format, replacing it
atomically every \-\-stats\-interval seconds (node_exporter textfile
collector). Env form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_STATS_FILE .
.TP
.BI run\ \fR--stats-interval\fP= SECONDS
Seconds between two publications of the aggregates (default 15, 1\-3600). Env
form:
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_STATS_INTERVAL .

.TP
.B run\ \fR--no-rate-limit\fP
Disable rate limiting entirely (benchmark/test seam; never in production \[em] it
removes the anti\-abuse limits the spec requires). Command\-line only: there is
deliberately no environment\-variable form, so a stale unit or environment file
cannot silently unthrottle a public node.
.TP
.B \fR--version\fP
Print the version and the protocol, then exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_IDENTITY
Path of the persistent TLS identity; same as
.BR "run \-\-identity" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_EPHEMERAL
Set to
.I 1
to keep the TLS identity in memory (no identity file); same as
.BR "run \-\-ephemeral" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_KEYS_FD
Integer file descriptor carrying the group keys; same as
.BR "run \-\-keys\-fd" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_KEYS_FILE
Path of the group-keys file; same as
.BR "run \-\-keys\-file" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_DEBUG
Set to
.I 1
to enable the full operation log on stderr (peer identity included); same as
.BR "run \-\-debug" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_READY_FILE
Path for the readiness PID file; same as
.BR "run \-\-ready\-file" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_PRIVATE
Set to
.I 1
to require a valid
.I bucket_token
on every frame (private node); same as
.BR "run \-\-private" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY
Set to
.I 0
to make the node discovery-only (the data relay stays off), or
.I 1
to force it on; same as
.BR "run \-\-relay" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY_IDLE_TIMEOUT
Seconds without traffic before a relayed session closes; same as
.BR "run \-\-relay\-idle\-timeout" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY_SESSION_BYTES
Per\-session forwarded\-byte ceiling; same as
.BR "run \-\-relay\-session\-bytes" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_RELAY_BANDWIDTH
Aggregate relay throughput cap in KiB/s; same as
.BR "run \-\-relay\-bandwidth" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_STATE_DIR
Installation state directory; the default identity lives under its
.IR rendezvous/
subdirectory.
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_HOST
Loopback address for the metrics endpoint; same as
.BR "run \-\-metrics\-host" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_PORT
Port of the loopback metrics endpoint; same as
.BR "run \-\-metrics\-port" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_TOKEN_FD
Descriptor holding the metrics bearer token; same as
.BR "run \-\-metrics\-token\-fd" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_METRICS_TOKEN_FILE
File holding the metrics bearer token; same as
.BR "run \-\-metrics\-token\-file" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_STATS_FILE
Prometheus text file for the aggregates; same as
.BR "run \-\-stats\-file" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_STATS_INTERVAL
Seconds between two aggregate publications; same as
.BR "run \-\-stats\-interval" .
.TP
.B HSYNC_VERSION
Overrides the version used by
.BR compile.sh (1)
when building executables and packages (per-language binaries live in
e.g. python/compile.sh).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/hsync-rendezvous/group-keys
Group keys for protected buckets, one
.I bucket=<hex\-key>
per line. The package creates it empty on install (an empty file means open
mode \(em no protected buckets); the administrator provisions the keys over it
(never part of the package; a root-only 0750 directory holds it). It is exposed
to the service only through the systemd credential mechanism (see
.BR HSYNC_RENDEZVOUS_KEYS_FD );
pass the keys with
.B \-\-keys\-file
instead when a service manager cannot inherit a descriptor (Windows).
.TP
.I /var/lib/hsync-rendezvous/identity.pem
Example explicit path for the persistent TLS identity (certificate + private
key, 0600), used with
.BR "run \-\-identity" ;
contains no peer data. Rotate it with
.BR "identity rotate" .
.B \-\-identity
accepts any writable path; the packaged systemd unit's StateDirectory gives the
DynamicUser sandbox a writable
.IR /var/lib/hsync-rendezvous
for the identity file, whose default location is
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/hsync/rendezvous/identity.pem .
.TP
.I /etc/hsync-rendezvous/metrics-token
Metrics bearer token (root\-only, 0600), exposed to the service as a systemd
credential. An empty file keeps the opt\-in endpoint disabled; the token must be
at least 32 characters.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hsync-rendezvous/stats.prom
Example target for
.BR "run \-\-stats\-file" :
inside the unit's StateDirectory, so the sandboxed process may replace it.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hsync-rendezvous.service
Hardened systemd unit (DynamicUser, ProtectSystem=strict, SystemCallFilter,
StandardOutput=null, MemoryMax). It is a
.B Type=notify
unit with a watchdog: it is considered started only once every QUIC/UDP
listener is bound, and it is restarted when the event loop stops answering,
not merely when the process dies. A graceful stop exits 0, so only a failure
triggers a restart. Its
.B MemoryMax=
is also the capacity ceiling: the node sizes its in\-memory limits from the memory
its cgroup allows. Installed but not enabled: opt in with
.B systemctl enable --now hsync-rendezvous.service.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/hsync-rendezvous
Standalone executable (symlink to
.IR /usr/lib/hsync-rendezvous/hsync-rendezvous ).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Private node with the keys read from a file (portable form):
.B hsync-rendezvous run --private --keys-file /etc/hsync-rendezvous/group-keys
.TP
Open mode (no protected buckets):
.B hsync-rendezvous run --listen-host 0.0.0.0 --listen-port 443
.TP
Listen on several UDP ports so clients whose egress firewall blocks UDP 443 can
reach the node on another port (443 QUIC + 53 DNS; 53 may require freeing the
local resolver):
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --listen-ports 443,53
.TP
Protected bucket with keys from a file descriptor:
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --protected-bucket shared --keys-fd 5 5</etc/hsync-rendezvous/group-keys
.TP
Private node (every bucket needs a key; no anonymous frames):
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --private --keys-fd 5 5</etc/hsync-rendezvous/group-keys
.TP
Show the certificate fingerprint printed at startup to pin the node on
clients:
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --listen-port 443
.TP
Read the persisted fingerprint to distribute to clients (the identity is created
on first run):
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous identity show
.TP
Run with an explicit identity path and a public SAN, then show the fingerprint:
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --identity /var/lib/hsync-rendezvous/identity.pem --public-host boot.example.com
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous identity show --identity /var/lib/hsync-rendezvous/identity.pem
.TP
Run a throwaway node that writes no identity file:
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --ephemeral
.TP
Rotate the identity after a suspected compromise:
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous identity rotate --identity /var/lib/hsync-rendezvous/identity.pem
.TP
Unprivileged port (no root or capability needed):
.br
.B hsync-rendezvous run --listen-port 8443
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal termination (Ctrl+C or SIGTERM).
.TP
.B 2
Configuration error (missing/duplicate group key, private node without a key,
out-of-range value): the node refuses to start.
.TP
.B 3
Listener bind failure: the QUIC/UDP port is privileged, already in use, or
.B --listen-host
is not a local address. The message on stderr explains the remediation.
.TP
.B 4
Readiness\-file failure: the path given to
.B run\ \fR--ready-file\fP
cannot be created (missing parent directory, no permission, or a stale file the
exclusive\-create seam refuses to overwrite).
.SH METRICS
The node exposes aggregate counters only \- never a CID, bucket, IP or port \- so
the no\-log invariant holds. Three sinks report the same numbers: the service
manager's
.B STATUS=
line, an optional Prometheus text file and an optional loopback endpoint.
.PP
The endpoint answers
.B GET /metrics
(and HEAD) on the configured loopback port, requires
.B Authorization: Bearer
and returns
.IR "text/plain; version=1.0.0; charset=utf-8" .
A missing or wrong token gets a uniform 401, any other path 404, any other method
405, a burst 429 and a concurrent request above the cap 503. The response carries
no peer identity and its cardinality is fixed.
.PP
The reported populations differ:
.B hsync_rendezvous_peers_current
and
.B hsync_rendezvous_peers_peak
count the clients registered right now and the highest level reached, while
.B hsync_rendezvous_quic_connections_current
and its
.I _peak
sibling count open QUIC connections \- handshake concurrency, not clients, because a
client opens one connection per exchange and lets it idle out after 30 seconds.
.B hsync_rendezvous_build_info
identifies the running build. Peaks are observed when the event happens, never
sampled, and never decrease; a restart resets them.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
Graceful shutdown: the transports are closed, the readiness file (if any) is
removed and the process exits with status 0. This is the stop path of every
service manager and of Ctrl+C.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Ignored. A hangup \- a terminal closed, an SSH session dropped, a PTY torn down
\- is not a stop request, so an unattended node keeps serving. There is no
configuration reload path: the identity and the group keys are read at startup,
so a replacement takes a restart.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
.P
